<Layout title="Languages for linked data and rules" description="How RDF, OWL, SKOS, SHACL and complementary vocabularies express different parts of a shared property model without confusing meaning, validation and evidence.">
  <h1>Languages for linked data and rules</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A useful model needs more than a hierarchy of things. It needs names people
    recognise, relationships they can explain, rules they can check and evidence
    they can inspect. We compose established languages around those needs, choosing
    each for the particular job it does well.
  </p>
  <p>
    The relevant source ODRs are normative for this approach within the selected
    ODR-0071 concerns. They define how we model; implementation coverage records how
    much has been delivered. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/decision-basis">decision
    basis</a> identifies that scope. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/standards">standards
    profile</a> records vocabulary choices, selected versions and actual candidate use
    separately; an unfinished implementation does not make a chosen method optional.
  </p>

  <h2 id="shared-foundation">RDF: one shared foundation</h2>
  <p>
    RDF represents statements as links: an identified inspection concerns an identified
    property; a report records that inspection. Identifiers let different documents
    discuss the same resource without depending on identical spelling or database
    column names. Literals hold values such as a date or a description. Turtle is a
    readable way to write that graph, not a separate model of the property world.
    See the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/">W3C RDF concepts</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A shared foundation makes vocabularies composable. It does not require one
    centrally imposed hierarchy. Nor does placing statements in a named graph, by
    itself, establish their authority or their semantic owner. Those responsibilities
    come from our <a href="/semantic-modelling/context-maps">context and mapping agreements</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="meaning">RDFS and OWL: express what the model means</h2>
  <p>
    Classes describe sets of things; properties describe relationships or values.
    A subclass assertion means every member of the narrower class also belongs to
    the wider class. It is a substantive claim, not just a convenient heading in a
    navigation tree. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/identity-roles-and-phases">identity,
    roles and phases guide</a> explains why a kind, a temporary role and a changing
    condition deserve different treatment.
  </p>
  <p>
    RDFS domain and range have consequences. If a property's domain is Inspection,
    using it implies that its subject is an Inspection. Giving that property two
    domains implies membership in <em>both</em> classes, not either one. Domain and
    range are not lists of allowed form fields. The
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf-schema/">RDFS specification</a> defines these rules.
  </p>
  <p>
    ODR-0014 therefore requires every governed property to document both subject and
    value applicability. Each side uses exactly one mechanism: a universally true
    single RDFS commitment, Schema.org inclusion hints for intended alternatives, or
    an explicit governed waiver. <a href="https://schema.org/domainIncludes"><code>schema:domainIncludes</code></a>
    and <code>schema:rangeIncludes</code> are the selected hints; they neither infer
    membership nor enforce constraints. They must not be combined with RDFS applicability
    or a waiver on the same side. Choosing not to run inference does not change what
    exported RDFS statements mean to other consumers.
  </p>
  <p>
    OWL adds formal expressions and axioms. Its open-world treatment means an absent
    value is not automatically false or invalid. ODR-0030 selects a bounded documentation
    profile: restrictions, Boolean class constructors, cardinality restrictions, closed
    OWL enumerations and keys are excluded. Their validation requirements belong in SHACL.
    The <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-syntax/">OWL specification</a> supplies the formal
    language; our chosen profile deliberately uses less than the complete language.
  </p>
  <p>
    ODR-0036 adds selective inference: class and property hierarchies, inverse links,
    transitivity, symmetry and disjointness checks. Domain/range, identity-merging
    constructs and equivalence propagation stay outside that runtime ruleset without
    losing their standard semantics. This is a chosen processing contract, not a
    claim of full OWL conformance or that every current candidate already executes it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vocabularies">SKOS: make controlled language usable</h2>
  <p>
    A controlled value needs a stable identity, definition, preferred label and often
    alternative terms. SKOS provides these together with concept schemes and
    broader, narrower and related relationships. A classification can therefore
    remain recognisable when its display label changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    A SKOS concept is not automatically an OWL class. <code>skos:broader</code> is
    not <code>rdfs:subClassOf</code> or an <code>isA</code> assertion, and it is not
    itself transitive. Its transitive counterpart supports ancestry through a
    concept hierarchy. These differences matter when a list becomes a navigable taxonomy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mapping strength matters too. <code>skos:exactMatch</code> links concepts; it does
    not assert individual identity with <code>owl:sameAs</code>, or class equivalence.
    Exact match is transitive; close match is not. Broad, narrow and related mappings
    preserve distinctions that an indiscriminate “same as” would erase. See the
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/skos-reference/">SKOS reference</a> and our
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/context-maps">context mapping approach</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="validation">SHACL: state what a particular delivery must contain</h2>
  <p>
    SHACL shapes can check datatypes, counts, classes, patterns and other supported
    constraints. A draft inspection record and a completed report may need different
    shapes while referring to the same underlying inspection. A delivery profile
    records which rules apply and why, without turning every delivery requirement
    into a universal assertion about the world.
  </p>
  <p>
    Conformance means the supplied graph met the supplied shapes under the declared
    processing assumptions. It does not prove that a building is safe, a statement
    is accurate or an author had authority. Those questions need evidence and review.
    Full RDFS inference is not required by SHACL; any required inference and supported
    features must be explicit. See the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/shacl/">SHACL specification</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Validation and derivation remain separate. ODR-0036 adopts SHACL Rules for producing
    derived triples, with rules held separately from constraints. Derived statements
    must remain distinguishable from asserted statements through graph separation or
    provenance. The selected rule features still need an implementation and execution
    record; a successful constraints check is not evidence that derivation ran.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">SPARQL: turn useful questions into executable queries</h2>
  <p>
    “Which reports describe this property?” and “Does this inspection have a recorded
    date?” are questions people can review before anyone writes a query. SPARQL
    expresses graph patterns to retrieve answers or check whether a pattern exists.
    Its <code>ASK</code> form returns a boolean; other forms retrieve bindings or
    construct graphs. These are language capabilities, not a claim that the current
    candidate uses them all. See the <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-query/">SPARQL query specification</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="example">An inspection, worked through</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose a surveyor inspects a property's roof and produces a report. The following
    example is illustrative, not an approved vocabulary or inspection requirement.
    Each concern adds information without collapsing the property, inspection and
    report into one object.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Different questions about the same inspection</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Modelling response</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">What are we describing?</th><td>Give the property, inspection and report separate identities; define their relationships.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">What does “urgent attention” mean?</th><td>Define a reviewed classification concept with a label and scope; link the relevant finding to it.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">What must this submitted record contain?</th><td>A delivery shape could require an inspection date and report reference if participants agree those rules.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Where did the conclusion come from?</th><td>Connect the report to the inspection, responsible agent and supporting source evidence.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Which information answers the buyer's question?</th><td>Query the relevant links and dates while retaining the source and scope of each answer.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A participant can challenge the classification, explain an exception or supply
    missing evidence in ordinary language. Learning the syntax is a task for the
    technical implementation, not a prerequisite for judging the meaning.
  </p>

  <h2 id="other-concerns">Reuse additional vocabularies for named concerns</h2>
  <p>
    Provenance, quality, time and sensitivity are part of the selected modelling
    concerns. Their normative decisions guide the choice of vocabulary and exact terms.
    The table explains complementary responsibilities, including catalogue and policy
    concerns; it does not imply wholesale imports or complete current implementation.
  </p>
  <table>
    <caption>Established vocabularies and their distinct responsibilities</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Vocabulary</th><th scope="col">What it contributes</th><th scope="col">Boundary to preserve</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.dublincore.org/specifications/dublin-core/dcmi-terms/">Dublin Core Terms</a></th><td>Descriptive metadata such as title, source and dates.</td><td>Which date or source relationship has the required meaning.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/prov-o/">PROV-O</a></th><td>Entities, activities, agents, attribution and derivation.</td><td>What evidence supports a claim; attribution alone does not establish accuracy.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/owl-time/">OWL-Time</a></th><td>Instants, intervals and temporal relationships.</td><td>Whether time describes validity, observation or recording.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-org/">ORG</a></th><td>Organisations, posts, roles and memberships.</td><td>Whether an organisational role is the relevant relationship, rather than a general role distinction.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-3/">DCAT</a></th><td>Dataset, distribution and service catalogue descriptions.</td><td>How a published package is discovered; its catalogue entry does not define property meaning.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dqv/">DQV</a></th><td>Quality measurements and annotations.</td><td>Which measures support fitness for a particular purpose.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row"><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/odrl-model/">ODRL</a></th><td>Permissions, prohibitions, duties and constraints.</td><td>How policy is interpreted and enforced; its description does not grant access.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="bounded-reuse">Compose deliberately, publish honestly</h2>
  <p>
    Referencing a standard, reusing a term, mapping to it and importing an ontology
    are different acts. An OWL import brings axioms from the import closure into
    scope; it is not a citation. Record the selected terms, version, dependencies
    and processing expectations. A familiar namespace prefix proves none of these.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shared model can support familiar projections: forms, dictionaries, APIs
    and documentation. Each needs a declared purpose and transformation; none should
    quietly become a competing definition of the underlying agreement. Start with the
    <a href="/semantic-modelling/standards">current standards profile</a> to distinguish
    this architecture from what has already been implemented and checked.
  </p>
</Layout>
